{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788317165,"nonce":"myq0PmzhwKLsS+MpF5bqhHubYXg=","title":"**There is no save verb, and that blocks a stated requirement.** Evan, setting up an unattended overnight run (m1-20260901, tick ~1,400,000): *\"I expect you to save right before every raid or conflict so I can see the base at its peak, and see the eventual fall from your records.\"*","message":"The protocol cannot do it. `rwa verbs` registers 100+ ops and none of them writes a save:\n\n```\n$ rwa verbs --json | jq -r '.data.verbs[]' | grep -i 'save\\|autosave'\n(nothing)\n```\n\n\nThe only save artifact on the bench is the game's own rotating autosave — `Autosave-1..5.rws`, five slots, cycling on `autosaveIntervalDays` (currently **1 in-game day**). So the driver copies the newest autosave out of `Saves/` before it rotates away:\n\n```bash\nN=$(ls -t \"$SAVES\"/Autosave-*.rws | head -1)\ncp \"$N\" \"$RUN/saves/threat-t${TICK}-${LABEL}.rws\"\n```\n\nThat is **the nearest autosave preceding the raid**, which on a 1-day interval can be up to 60,000 ticks stale. \"The base at its peak, immediately before the fight\" is exactly what it cannot give. The run's saves are labelled `nearest autosave; no save verb exists` for that reason, rather than pretending otherwise.\n\nTwo workarounds were considered and rejected:\n\n- **Shortening `autosaveIntervalDays` in `Prefs.xml`.** `Prefs` is read into memory at startup; rewriting the file mid-session does not move `Prefs.AutosaveIntervalDays`, so it would need a relaunch — and a relaunch mid-run is a bench change the loop is told not to make on its own (`PLAY-LOOP.md` §Escalation).\n- **Waiting for an autosave to land before advancing into the threat.** The threat letter has already arrived by the time the loop sees it; the fight is inside the advance that carried it. There is no \"before\" left to wait for.\n\n\n- **It is the only way to make a run's evidence re-runnable.** `postmortem.md`'s whole method is reading the state around an event; `rwa journal` can be read after the game exits, but a *save* is the only artifact that lets a later session stand in the colony and look. M1's own post-mortem read `saves/Autosave-5.rws` for the wealth series precisely because the journal could not answer it.\n- **`664e9b9`'s deliverables list says \"the saves\"** and there is no verb that produces one. Every run so far has quietly meant \"whatever autosave happened to be lying around\".\n- **A save is the natural checkpoint for an unattended run.** The overnight driver already saves at every day boundary and every threat; both are copies of something the game decided to write, not of the moment the loop cared about.\n- It pairs with the halt vocabulary already shipped. `advance` halts on `threat` and on `casualty`; a `save` verb would let the loop do `halt -\u003e save -\u003e read -\u003e act` and give a post-mortem an exact pre-event snapshot.\n\n\n1. A `save {name}` verb writes a named save into the bench's `Saves/` and returns the path, the tick and the sid. It journals as an `action` like any other mutating verb — a save is a real side effect on disk and the transcript should show when the run took one.\n2. It does not disturb the autosave rotation (do not consume a rotating slot), and it refuses rather than silently overwriting an existing name unless `overwrite:true`.\n3. It is callable while paused and while an advance is NOT in flight; a `busy` refusal is fine and matches the existing convention.\n4. `PLAY-LOOP.md` §Artifacts gains the save as a named artifact with the moments it is owed at: every threat halt, every casualty halt, every day boundary in an unattended run.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-save.py` on a bench: save, assert the file exists with the right name, assert the returned tick matches `digest.time.tick`, assert the autosave slots were untouched, assert a duplicate name is refused and `overwrite:true` accepted.\n\n\n`GameDataSaveLoader.SaveGame` is the vanilla call and it is one line. The read half of this — loading — is deliberately outside the protocol (`PLAY-LOOP.md` position 6: \"loading is the LAUNCHER's job\"), and that asymmetry is fine and should be stated in the verb's own header: **the agent may write a save; only the launcher may load one.**","files":null}]}